    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d368">

                <pb facs="0532" n="420" xml:id="pg420"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/7382: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Counselor of Embassy in China</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Lockhart</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-11-16T12:00:00+08:00">November 16,
                        1935—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received November 16—9 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>151. Embassy’s 150, November 15, 9 p.m.<anchor n="43" xml:id="d368-f1"/> Reports
                    of fresh arrival of Japanese troops at Shanhaikwan are confirmed, the number
                    being given as approximately 3,000. No troops have moved westward from
                    Shanhaikwan and it may be of some significance that some of them have already
                    been billeted, indicating that it is not their immediate intention of proceeding
                    westward. However, an unconfirmed report has just been received that the
                    Japanese have ordered in “Manchukuo” 10 trains for movement of troops. Inquiry
                    is being made of the Consulate General at Mukden.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Nanking, Shanghai and Tokyo; by mail to Mukden.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Lockhart</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
